<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">U Thant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1909–1974</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">U Thant was a Burmese diplomat who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-general-of-the-united-nations">Secretary-General of the United Nations</a> from 1961 to 1971, the first Asian to hold the post. A teacher and friend of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-nu">U Nu</a>, he took office after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dag-hammarskjold">Dag Hammarskjöld</a> and helped mediate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a> of 1962. His funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> in 1974 led to student protests against military rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/u-thant.jpg" alt="U Thant" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jack de Nijs for Anefo / Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U_Thant_(1963).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
